  "strategy": "We propose a hierarchical optimization approach that uses a multi-resolution strategy and structural modifications to escape local minima. The solution leverages coarse to fine optimization to discover patterns that minimize overlap at different scales while maintaining the integral constraint. The key differences from the previous approach are:\n\n1. Uses hierarchical optimization from coarse to fine grid\n2. Implements structural modifications through wavelet-like filtering\n3. Projects parent's characteristics to all resolutions\n4. Uses gradient-based optimization with global steps\n\n1.Approach and Rationale:\nThe core idea is to use the hierarchical approach to generalize patterns observed across scales. We first optimize a coarse grid pattern that minimizes overlap, then refine it to higher resolution through interpolation, scaling, and structural filtering. Structural modifications (adding/moving features as wavelets) allow us to explore topologically different solutions while maintaining feasibility. The rationale is that minimal overlap patterns may be anchored to fundamental structures independent of resolution. The approach:\n- Maintains better global exploration while reducing local search time\n- Transfers fixed-point characteristics across resolutions\n- Uses gradient-based steps away from local minima\n- Cares about very small improvements through geometric projection\n\nPotentially addresses bottlenecks of excessive local search and limited escape from suboptimal fixed-points seen in coarse-grid-only approaches.\n\n2.Mathematical Specification:\nVariables:\n- h: Solution vector (length n_points) representing binary coverage\n- sum_constraint = n_points/2 (integral constraint)\n- C5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx (overlap measure)\n\nWe model the solution as:\nh^(0) = initial guess (projected parent)\n\nThen we optimize in three physical steps:\n1. Coarse optimization (n_coarse=104 points)\n   - C5_coarse = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx (with dx = 2/104)\n   - Constraint: \u2211h_i = 52 (since 52 * 0.0192 = 1)\n\n2. Refinement to full resolution (n_points=280)\n   - Use interpolation and scaling\n   - Apply wavelet-style structural filtering\n\n3. Structural modification (gradient-based adjustments)\n   - Add synthetic features (wavelets) at strategic positions\n\nKey formulations:\n- Wavelet overlay operator\n- Projection operator to maintain integral\n- Modified objective with closest point interpolation\n\nThe solver handles:\n- Box constraints: 0<=h_i<=1\n- Integral constraint\n- Local modifications preserved through structured updates\n\n3.Implementation Procedure:\nWe implement the following stages:\n\n3.1 Input Handling:\n- Read initial_h_values (if provided)\n- Set working resolution: multi-scale (n_coarse=104, n_fine=280)\n\n3.2 Coarse Grid Optimization:\n- Lower-resolution grid suitable for global search\n- Objective: minimize C5_coarse\n- Constraints: integral=52\n- Projection: parent available? (adjust and reuse)\n\n3.3 Fine Grid Optimization:\n- Interpolate and scale to full resolution\n- Apply structural filtering via wavelet overlays\n- Re-formulate constraint in fine space\n\n3.4 Wavelet-based Structural Modification:\n- Add/remove features via operator in Fourier domain\n- Apply targeted coverage adjustments\n- Gradient descent with Lipschitz stabilization\n\n3.5 Feasibility Maintenance:\n- Post-processing clipping\n- Corrective scaling\n- Decay gradient steps as converge\n\nEach stage handles input processing and output validation. Key operations include:\n\n- Interpolation: np.interp\n- Fast Fourier transform for wavelet analysis (np.fft)\n- Constraint projection using backtracking line search\n- Bounds repair using exponential penalty (for large violations)\n\n4.Search Choices and Variations:\nCore configuration:\n- n_coarse=104 (for optimal architectural preservations)\n- n_fine=280 (primary domain)\n- Coarse_opt_repetitions=20\n- Fine_opt_iterations=10 (local adjustment)\n\nKey variation:\n0: Base case coloring density preserving\n1: Wavelet overlay depth (0.1 to 1.0)\n2: Gradient descent variant (momentum, adaptive)\n\nAlgorithm structure follows a nested optimization where each coarse pattern seeds a fine solution, which is augmented with structural elements to exceed asymptotic limits.\n\n5.Compute Budget and Robustness:\nEstimation for n_points=280:\n- FFT cost: O(n log n) ~ 28 * 160 operations per FFT\n- Constraint projection: O(n) ~ 140 operations\n- Gradient calculation: sparse directional (average 7 per step)\n\nAssuming 40 gradient steps per refinement, total operations: ~ 280 * log(280) * 40 * (overheads). On a basic machine, expect 2-5s per coarse refinement, with final cleanup. We must stagger computing groups to avoid large peaks.\n\nFallback if optimization fails:\n- Use best seen comprehensive projection\n- If parent not available, then fallback to (140) uniform pattern\n\n6.Validation and Return Contract:\nMust pass:\n- 0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i\n- \u2211h = 140\n- Multiple measurements (if time allows)\n\nFinal solution must be re-evaluated using:\n- np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full') * dx\n- C5 = max_value of the correlation\n\nReturn format (h_values, c5_bound, n_points) as required.\n\nThe coder must implement all steps with care to maintain the integral constraint precisely during structural modifications and properly handle floating point range.\n\nWe provide complete pseudocode implementation principles within the implementation block.",
